TERMINOLOGY:

Ace — highest or lowest card depending on game. — As
King — face card ranked below Ace. — Rey
Queen — face card. — Reina
Jack — face card. — Jota / Sota
Rank — card value (A,2–10,J,Q,K). — Valor / Rango
Suit — one of clubs ♣, diamonds ♦, hearts ♥, spades ♠. — Palo
Deck — standard 52-card pack. — Baraja / Mazo
Shuffle — randomize the deck. — Barajar
Cut — split the deck (usually before dealing). — Cortar (la baraja)
Deal — distribute cards to players or board. — Repartir



Hole cards / Pocket cards — player’s private cards — Cartas ocultas
Community cards — shared face-up cards on the table (flop/turn/river). — Cartas comunitarias
Flop — first three community cards. — Flop (o las tres cartas)
Turn — fourth community card. — Turn (cuarta carta)
River — fifth community card. — River (quinta carta)
Board — the current set of community cards. — Mesa / Tablero
Hand — the set of cards used to make a poker combination. — Mano
Hand ranking — ordering of poker hands (best → worst). — Jerarquía de manos
Royal flush — A-K-Q-J-10 same suit. — Escalera real
Straight flush — five consecutive of same suit. — Escalera de color
Four of a kind (quads) — four same rank. — Poker / Cuatro iguales
Full house (boat) — three of a kind + a pair. — Full / Full house
Flush — five same suit, not consecutive. — Color
Straight — five consecutive ranks. — Escalera
Three of a kind (set/trips) — three same rank. — Trío / Tercia
Two pair — two different pairs. — Doble pareja
One pair — a single pair. — Pareja
High card — no pair; highest card wins. — Carta alta
Kicker — side card used to break ties. — Kicker / Carta de desempate
Nuts — the best possible hand given the board. — La mejor mano posible / “nuts”
Outs — cards that improve a hand. — Outs / Cartas que ayudan
Pot — total chips/money bet this hand. — Pozo / Bote
Stack — player’s chips. — Stack / Fichas
Blind (small blind / big blind) — forced bets in games like Hold’em. — Ciega pequeña / ciega grande
Dealer button — marker for dealer position. — Botón del dealer / botón
Position — order relative to dealer (early/middle/late). — Posición (temprana/Media/tardía)
Check — pass without betting if no bet to call. — Pasar / Check
Bet — place chips into pot. — Apostar
Call — match the current bet. — Igualar / Meter
Raise — increase the bet. — Subir (la apuesta)
Fold — give up the hand. — Retirarse / Foldear
All-in — bet all your chips. — Ir all-in / Apostar todo
Bluff — bet with a weak hand to make others fold. — Farol / Bluff
Value bet — bet to extract value from worse hands. — Apuesta de valor
Showdown — reveal hands after final betting to decide winner. — Showdown / Mostrar las cartas
Muck — discard cards face-down (usually after folding). — Muck / Descartar
Rake — house take from pot. — Comisión / Rake

All-in – Betting all of one’s remaining chips in a hand.
Ante – A small forced bet from every player before a hand begins (common in tournaments).
Bad beat – Losing a hand despite having very strong cards, usually when an opponent catches a lucky draw.
Bet – To put chips into the pot as a wager.
Betting round – One of several intervals in a hand when players can bet, check, raise, or fold (preflop, flop, turn, river).
Big blind – A forced bet posted by the player two seats left of the dealer button.
Blank – A community card that seems unlikely to improve anyone’s hand.
Bluff – To bet or raise with a weak hand, hoping opponents fold.
Board – The set of community cards in games like Texas Hold’em or Omaha.
Bring in – The forced first bet in stud poker (made by the player with the lowest upcard).
Button – A disk indicating the dealer position (rotates clockwise after each hand).
Buy-in – The minimum amount of money required to sit at a table or enter a tournament.
Call – To match the current bet amount.
Check – To pass the action without betting (only allowed if no bet has been made in that round).
Check-raise – Checking initially, then raising after an opponent bets.
Cold call – Calling both a bet and a raise (or multiple raises) in the same action.
Community cards – Cards dealt face-up in the center of the table, shared by all players.
Connectors – Two consecutive-ranking cards (e.g., 8♠ 9♠).
Cripple – To hold a hand so strong it “breaks” the deck’s possibilities (e.g., holding both remaining aces in a deck).
Cut / Cut the deck – To split the deck into two parts before dealing (usually after a shuffle).
Deal – To distribute cards to players.
Dealer – The person or button position responsible for distributing cards.
Deck – The full set of 52 playing cards.
Disconnect protection – In online poker, a feature that saves a player’s hand if their connection drops.
Draw – Needing specific cards to complete a hand (e.g., flush draw, straight draw).
Early position – Players who act first in a betting round (left of the blinds).
Equity – A player’s mathematical chance of winning a hand at a given moment.
Face card – King, Queen, or Jack.
Fifth street – Another name for the river (final community card).
Fish – A weak or inexperienced player.
Flop – The first three community cards in Hold’em/Omaha; also the betting round after they appear.
Flush – Five cards of the same suit.
Fold – To quit the current hand (forfeit any chips already bet that round).
Four of a kind – Four cards of the same rank (quads).
Free card – Getting to see another street without having to call a bet (often by checking).
Full house – Three of a kind plus a pair.
Heads-up – Play between only two players.
Hole cards – Private cards dealt face-down to each player.
Kicker – The unpaired, highest side card in a hand (used to break ties).
Late position – Players who act later in a betting round (closer to the button), giving them an advantage.
Limp – To call the big blind instead of raising preflop.
Live cards – In stud, cards not yet seen that can improve one’s hand; also, cards that are not dead.
Muck – The discard pile; also, to fold or throw away cards face-down.
Nuts – The best possible hand given the board.
Odds – The probability of completing a drawing hand, or the ratio of risk to reward.
Offsuit – Cards of different suits (e.g., A♣ K♥).
Open – To make the first bet in a round.
Outs – Remaining cards that can improve a player’s hand to a likely winner.
Overcard – A hole card higher than any community card.
Pair – Two cards of the same rank.
Pocket – A player’s two hole cards in Hold’em/Omaha (e.g., pocket aces).
Position – A player’s order relative to the dealer button (early, middle, late).
Pot – The total chips bet in a hand.
Pot odds – The ratio of the current pot size to the cost of a contemplated call.
Preflop – Betting round before any community cards are dealt.
Push – To shove all-in; also, to slide chips into the pot.
Quads – Four of a kind.
Raise – To increase the current bet amount.
Rake – The house’s commission from each cash game pot.
Rank – The value of a card (Ace, King, Queen, Jack, 10, 9, …, 2).
Riffle – A common shuffling method that interlaces two halves of the deck.
River – The fifth and final community card; also the last betting round.
Rock – A very tight player who rarely plays hands.
Royal flush – A♥ K♥ Q♥ J♥ 10♥ (ace-high straight flush).
Runner-runner – Making a hand by hitting needed cards on both the turn and river.
Set – Three of a kind when you hold a pocket pair.
Shuffle – To randomize the order of cards in the deck.
Showdown – All remaining players reveal their hole cards to determine the winner.
Side pot – A separate pot created when one or more players are all-in.
Slow play – To check or call with a strong hand to trap opponents.
Small blind – The smaller forced bet posted by the player immediately left of the dealer button.
Smooth call – Call
